For thread, cotton, fermented dregs, cow-dung, and molasses;
for curds, milk, buttermilk, water, and fruit;
for bamboo and split-reed utensils, and likewise salts;
for all earthenware, for earth, and for ash;

those who know dharma and speak the truth declare it a transgression of the vow.
To achieve purification for this very [sin], wearing the skin of a donkey,
he should beg alms at seven houses, proclaiming his own deed;
subsisting once a day on the alms obtained from them,
and bathing at the three junctures of the day, he is purified in a year.
Having killed an unknown fetus, he should perform the observance for killing a brahmin;
as also for [killing] a kṣatriya or vaiśya who has sacrificed, or a woman who has bathed after her courses,
or having spoken falsely in testimony, or having contradicted teachers,
or having misappropriated a deposit, or having killed a woman or a friend.
A twice-born who has drunk liquor through delusion should drink fire-hot liquor;
when his body is consumed by it, he is freed from that sin;
or he should drink fire-hot cow's urine, or water,
or milk, or clarified butter until death, or the juice of cow dung;
or for a year, wearing rags, with matted hair, carrying a staff [topped with a skull],
he should eat broken grains or oil-cake once at night to dispel the drinking of liquor.
Liquor is indeed the impurity of foods, and sin is called impurity;

A thief is purified by death, but a brahmin through asceticism alone.
Wishing to dispel through asceticism the stain arising from the theft of gold,
a twice-born, clad in rags, should perform the observance for a slayer of a brahmin in the forest.
He who has violated his guru's bed, having confessed it, should lie down on an iron bed;
embracing a glowing iron effigy, he is purified by death;
or having himself cut off his penis and testicles and placed them in his cupped hands,
he should walk straight toward the south-west until he falls dead;
or carrying a skull-topped staff, clad in rags, bearded, in a lonely forest,
he should attentively perform the prājāpatya kṛcchra penance for one year;
or with senses restrained, he should practice the cāndrāyaṇa for three months,
with sacrificial food and barley gruel, for the removal of [the sin of] violating the guru's bed.
Having intentionally performed an act causing loss of caste,
he should perform the sāntapana kṛcchra penance, but if unintentionally, the prājāpatya.
In acts leading to a mixed state, the lunar penance is the purification;

or bathe in a flowing river, or chant the threefold hymn. / Having killed a snake, the best of the twice-born should give an iron spade;
for an eunuch, a load of straw and a māṣaka of lead; / for a boar, a pot of ghee; and for a partridge, a droṇa of sesame seeds;
for a parrot, a two-year-old calf; having killed a krauñca, a two-year-old [calf]; / having killed a swan, a crane, a heron, or a peacock,
a monkey, a falcon, or a vulture, he should present a cow to a brahmin. / Having killed a horse, he should give a garment; for an elephant, five dark bulls;
having killed a goat, a sheep, an ox, or a donkey, a one-year-old [calf]; / having killed a carnivorous doe, he should give a milch cow;
for a non-carnivorous one, a heifer; but having killed a camel, a kṛṣṇala. / For [killing] animals such as deer, bow-carrying animals, or calves, he should give each separately for purification.
Having killed unintentionally a woman of any of the four varnas, / [or] of the three varnas in regular order, without distinction,

having bathed and not eaten for one day, greeting respectfully, he should propitiate [him]. / Having struck [him] even with a blade of grass, or bound [him] by the neck with a cloth,
or even having defeated [him] in an argument, bowing down, he should propitiate [him]. / Threatening [a brahmin] with intent to kill, he attains hell for a hundred years,
and for a thousand [years] by striking [him]. / As many dust-particles as the blood of a twice-born binds together,
for that many thousands of years the perpetrator goes to hell. / For threatening, he should perform a kṛcchra, an atikṛcchra for striking down;
he should perform a kṛcchrātikṛcchra upon drawing blood from a brahmin. / A Caṇḍāla or the like, unknown, who should stay in someone's dwelling,
when properly identified in time, he should perform its purification. / A cāndrāyaṇa or a parāka is the purification for the twice-born,
while a prājāpatya is for śūdras, and the rest in accordance with that. / Jaggery, safflower, salt, and whatever grains [there are],
having placed [them] in the house, he should then apply fire to them at the door. / But for earthen vessels, discarding alone is prescribed;

2.17.m:
Ending in 'śarman', 'varman', 'dhana' or 'artha', and 'dāsa' respectively,
the name should be given, or in accordance with one's family.
For women, it should be easy to pronounce, not harsh, of clear meaning, and charming;
the name, O noble one, should be given, and it should not have an uneven number of syllables.
Having named the child, he should present him to the mace-bearing God after worship,
O noble one, saying: 'Indeed, this is your son.'
A student, a servant, an attendant, and one who receives a share is Keśava;
Madhusūdana is always present through an auspicious distribution.
Then worship of the brahmins should be performed according to rule;
and here he should feed the brahmins with rice-milk accompanied by a sacrificial fee.
Then the tonsure-rite should be performed, O son of Bhṛgu, according to family custom;
lunar mansions here are recommended that are soft, swift, and movable.
He should avoid an empty lunar day, as well as the days of the Sun and Mars;
having performed worship there especially to Vāsudeva.
